          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows love for St John, explicitly characterizing it as sisterly rather than romantic love. The qualification narrows the kind of affection but does not weaken the target's truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not undertake marriage, travel, service, or any new obligation. Its surrounding turn expressly denies an obligation to go to India; Jane's love explains what she would have risked, rather than committing her now to do it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a controlled explanation of Jane's position and reasons. It is an avowal, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately represented as a truth-presenting avowal of sisterly affection. No additional core function distinct from T, P, and E is supported.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is resisting St John's claim that she has promised or is obliged to go to India. She explains that her willingness to risk much with him arose from admiration, trust, and sisterly love, while rejecting marriage and denying any present obligation to accompany him.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression makes no undertaking. In the surrounding exchange Jane denies being obligated to go to India and maintains her refusal to become St John's wife. St John does not accept the sisterly avowal as resolving their disagreement; he responds by accusing her of fear and condemning her attachment to Mr Rochester.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports Jane's stated sisterly affection and the absence of undertaking force in the focal words. It does not independently establish every aspect of her inner sincerity or the full history of the relationship beyond what is represented here.\"}"
        }
